<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thebes</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Waset, Diospolis, City of Amun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, known to the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> as Waset, was a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> about 800 kilometres south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, whose ruins lie within the modern city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a>. The main city of the fourth Upper Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nome</a>, it was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> for long periods of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Kingdom</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> and a leading religious centre, known from the end of the New Kingdom as the City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>. The temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> and Luxor stand on the east bank, and the west bank holds a necropolis of royal and private tombs. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> called it Diospolis, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> Thebes of the Hundred Gates. Its ruins became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1979.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thebes-egypt.jpg" alt="Thebes" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rémih · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luxor_temple27.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
